#extend("base"):
    #export("content"):
        <article class="information-page">
            <header class="page-heading">
                <p class="state-label">Ephemeral</p>
                <h1>Service information</h1>
                <p>Ephemeral lets you upload a file and share it through a temporary download link. No account is required. Files are automatically removed after they expire or reach their download limit.</p>
            </header>

            <dl class="service-facts">
                <div>
                    <dt>Maximum file size</dt>
                    <dd>#(maximumFileSizeLabel)</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Available for</dt>
                    <dd>Up to #(storageDurationLabel)</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Download limit</dt>
                    <dd>#(downloadLimitLabel)</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <section class="information-section">
                <h2>What happens to your file</h2>
                <p>After uploading a file, you receive a link that you can share with others. Each completed download uses one of the link’s available downloads.</p>
                <p>The file is deleted after its final allowed download or when its retention period ends, whichever happens first. Incomplete and expired uploads are also removed automatically.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="information-section">
                <h2>What the service stores</h2>
                <p>Ephemeral stores your file and the information needed to deliver it, including its filename, type, size, upload time, and remaining download count.</p>
                <p>The service does not require an account or create a personal file library.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="information-section">
                <h2>Keeping your link secure</h2>
                <p>Uploads require a verification check to reduce automated abuse. Anyone with the download link can access the file while it remains available, so share the link only with its intended recipients.</p>
                <p>Files are not end-to-end encrypted. The service operator can technically access stored files, and HTTPS protects files while they travel between your browser and the service.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="information-section">
                <h2>Limitations</h2>
                <p>Only one file can be uploaded at a time. Downloads cannot be paused or resumed, and an interrupted download may still use one of the link’s available downloads.</p>
            </section>

            <a class="button button--dark" href="/">Share a file</a>
        </article>
    #endexport
#endextend
